title: Vite Plugin
id: vite-plugin
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Vite Plugin for TanStack Devtools provides a seamless integration for using the devtools in your Vite-powered applications. With this plugin, you get complementary features on top of the\nexisting features built into the devtools like better console logs, server event bus, and enhanced debugging capabilities."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To add the devtools vite plugin you need to install it as a development dependency:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install -D @tanstack/devtools-vite","lang":"sh"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then add it as the "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"FIRST"}]},{"type":"text","value":" plugin in your Vite config:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools(),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And you're done!"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"configuration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can configure the devtools plugin by passing options to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"devtools"},{"type":"text","value":" function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      // options here\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"eventbusconfig","children":[{"type":"text","value":"eventBusConfig"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration for the event bus that the devtools use to communicate with the client"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      eventBusConfig: {\n        // port to run the event bus on\n        port: 1234,\n        // console log debug logs or not\n        debug: false,\n        // enables the server event bus (defaults to true), you can disable it if you're running devtools in something like storybook or vitest\n        enabled: true\n      },\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"editor","children":[{"type":"text","value":"editor"}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"editor"},{"type":"text","value":" is used as an escape hatch to implement your own go-to-source functionality if your system/editor does not work OOTB. We use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"launch-editor"},{"type":"text","value":" under the hood which supports a lot of editors but not all. If your editor is not supported you can implement your own version here. Here is the list of supported editors: https://github.com/yyx990803/launch-editor?tab=readme-ov-file#supported-editors"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The open in editor configuration which has two fields, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"open"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" is the name of your editor, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"open"},{"type":"text","value":" is a function that opens the editor with the given file and line number. You can implement your version for your editor as follows:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n plugins: [\n   devtools({\n     editor: {\n       name: 'VSCode',\n       open: async (path, lineNumber, columnNumber) => {\n         const { exec } = await import('node:child_process')\n         exec(\n           // or windsurf/cursor/webstorm\n           `code -g \"${(path).replaceAll('$', '\\\\$')}${lineNumber ? `:${lineNumber}` : ''}${columnNumber ? `:${columnNumber}` : ''}\"`,\n         )\n       },\n     },\n   }),\n   // ... rest of your plugins here\n ],\n}\n","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enhancedlogs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"enhancedLogs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration for enhanced logging. Defaults to enabled."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      enhancedLogs: {\n        enabled: true\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"removedevtoolsonbuild","children":[{"type":"text","value":"removeDevtoolsOnBuild"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether to remove devtools from the production build. Defaults to true."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      removeDevtoolsOnBuild: true\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"logging","children":[{"type":"text","value":"logging"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Whether to log information to the console. Defaults to true."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      logging: true\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"injectsource","children":[{"type":"text","value":"injectSource"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration for source injection. Defaults to enabled."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      injectSource: {\n        enabled: true,\n        ignore: {\n          // files to ignore source injection for\n          files: ['node_modules', /.*\\.test\\.(js|ts|jsx|tsx)$/],\n          // components to ignore source injection for\n          components: ['YourComponent', /.*Lazy$/],\n        },\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"consolepiping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"consolePiping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Configuration for bidirectional console piping between client and server. When enabled, console logs from the client will appear in your terminal, and server logs will appear in the browser console. Defaults to enabled."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      consolePiping: {\n        // Whether to enable console piping (defaults to true)\n        enabled: true,\n        // Which console methods to pipe (defaults to all)\n        levels: ['log', 'warn', 'error', 'info', 'debug'],\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"features","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Vite plugin is composed of several sub-plugins, each handling a specific concern:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"graph TD\n    vite[\"@tanstack/devtools-vite\"]\n    vite --> source[\"Source Injection<br/><i>AST → data-tsd-source attrs</i>\"]\n    vite --> server[\"Server Event Bus<br/><i>WebSocket + SSE transport</i>\"]\n    vite --> strip[\"Production Stripping<br/><i>Remove devtools on build</i>\"]\n    vite --> pipe[\"Console Piping<br/><i>Client ↔ Server logs</i>\"]\n    vite --> logs[\"Enhanced Logs<br/><i>Source location in console</i>\"]\n    vite --> market[\"Marketplace<br/><i>Install plugins via UI</i>\"]\n    vite --> connect[\"Connection Injection<br/><i>Port/host placeholders</i>\"]","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"go-to-source","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Go to Source"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The \"Go to Source\" feature lets you click on any element in your browser and open its source file in your editor at the exact line where it's defined. It works by injecting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data-tsd-source"},{"type":"text","value":" attributes into your components via an AST transformation during development. These attributes encode the file path and line number of each element."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To use it, activate the source inspector by holding the inspect hotkey (default: Shift+Alt+Ctrl/Meta). An overlay will highlight elements under your cursor and display their source location. Clicking on a highlighted element opens the corresponding file in your editor at the exact line, powered by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"launch-editor"},{"type":"text","value":" under the hood."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a complete guide on configuration and usage, see the "},{"type":"link","href":"./source-inspector","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source Inspector"}]},{"type":"text","value":" docs."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"console-piping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Console Piping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When enabled (default), "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"console.log()"},{"type":"text","value":" and other console methods in the browser are piped to your terminal, and server-side console output appears in the browser console. This is particularly useful when debugging SSR or API routes — you see all logs in one place without switching between terminal and browser. Configure which log levels are piped via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"consolePiping.levels"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enhanced-logs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Enhanced Logs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Console logs are enhanced with clickable source locations. In the browser console, each log shows the file and line number where it originated. Click to open the source file in your editor. Enable/disable via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enhancedLogs"},{"type":"text","value":" config option."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"production-build-stripping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production Build Stripping"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"removeDevtoolsOnBuild: true"},{"type":"text","value":"), the Vite plugin replaces all devtools imports with empty modules in production builds. This includes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-devtools"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/vue-devtools"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/solid-devtools"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/preact-devtools"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/devtools"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This ensures zero devtools code reaches production. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"removeDevtoolsOnBuild: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to keep devtools in production (see "},{"type":"link","href":"./production","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production"}]},{"type":"text","value":" docs)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"plugin-marketplace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Plugin Marketplace"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Vite plugin enables the in-devtools plugin marketplace. When you browse available plugins in the devtools Settings tab and click \"Install\", the Vite plugin handles the npm/pnpm/yarn installation and automatically injects the plugin import into your devtools setup file. This only works during development with the Vite dev server running."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"configuration","text":"Configuration","level":2},{"id":"eventbusconfig","text":"eventBusConfig","level":3},{"id":"editor","text":"editor","level":3},{"id":"enhancedlogs","text":"enhancedLogs","level":3},{"id":"removedevtoolsonbuild","text":"removeDevtoolsOnBuild","level":3},{"id":"logging","text":"logging","level":3},{"id":"injectsource","text":"injectSource","level":3},{"id":"consolepiping","text":"consolePiping","level":3},{"id":"features","text":"Features","level":2},{"id":"go-to-source","text":"Go to Source","level":3},{"id":"console-piping","text":"Console Piping","level":3},{"id":"enhanced-logs","text":"Enhanced Logs","level":3},{"id":"production-build-stripping","text":"Production Build Stripping","level":3},{"id":"plugin-marketplace","text":"Plugin Marketplace","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe Vite Plugin for TanStack Devtools provides a seamless integration for using the devtools in your Vite-powered applications. With this plugin, you get complementary features on top of the\nexisting features built into the devtools like better console logs, server event bus, and enhanced debugging capabilities.\n\n## Installation\n\nTo add the devtools vite plugin you need to install it as a development dependency:\n\n```sh\nnpm install -D @tanstack/devtools-vite\n```\n\nThen add it as the *FIRST* plugin in your Vite config:\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools(),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\nAnd you're done!\n\n## Configuration\n\nYou can configure the devtools plugin by passing options to the `devtools` function:\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      // options here\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### eventBusConfig\n\n  Configuration for the event bus that the devtools use to communicate with the client\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      eventBusConfig: {\n        // port to run the event bus on\n        port: 1234,\n        // console log debug logs or not\n        debug: false,\n        // enables the server event bus (defaults to true), you can disable it if you're running devtools in something like storybook or vitest\n        enabled: true\n      },\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n\n```\n\n### editor\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] \n> `editor` is used as an escape hatch to implement your own go-to-source functionality if your system/editor does not work OOTB. We use `launch-editor` under the hood which supports a lot of editors but not all. If your editor is not supported you can implement your own version here. Here is the list of supported editors: https://github.com/yyx990803/launch-editor?tab=readme-ov-file#supported-editors\n\nThe open in editor configuration which has two fields, `name` and `open`,\n`name` is the name of your editor, and `open` is a function that opens the editor with the given file and line number. You can implement your version for your editor as follows:\n\n ```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      editor: {\n        name: 'VSCode',\n        open: async (path, lineNumber, columnNumber) => {\n          const { exec } = await import('node:child_process')\n          exec(\n            // or windsurf/cursor/webstorm\n            `code -g \"${(path).replaceAll('$', '\\\\$')}${lineNumber ? `:${lineNumber}` : ''}${columnNumber ? `:${columnNumber}` : ''}\"`,\n          )\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n\n```\n\n### enhancedLogs\n\n  Configuration for enhanced logging. Defaults to enabled.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      enhancedLogs: {\n        enabled: true\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### removeDevtoolsOnBuild\n\nWhether to remove devtools from the production build. Defaults to true.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      removeDevtoolsOnBuild: true\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### logging\n  Whether to log information to the console. Defaults to true.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      logging: true\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### injectSource\n\nConfiguration for source injection. Defaults to enabled.\n\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      injectSource: {\n        enabled: true,\n        ignore: {\n          // files to ignore source injection for\n          files: ['node_modules', /.*\\.test\\.(js|ts|jsx|tsx)$/],\n          // components to ignore source injection for\n          components: ['YourComponent', /.*Lazy$/],\n        },\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### consolePiping\n\nConfiguration for bidirectional console piping between client and server. When enabled, console logs from the client will appear in your terminal, and server logs will appear in the browser console. Defaults to enabled.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      consolePiping: {\n        // Whether to enable console piping (defaults to true)\n        enabled: true,\n        // Which console methods to pipe (defaults to all)\n        levels: ['log', 'warn', 'error', 'info', 'debug'],\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n## Features\n\nThe Vite plugin is composed of several sub-plugins, each handling a specific concern:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    vite[\"@tanstack/devtools-vite\"]\n    vite --> source[\"Source Injection<br/><i>AST → data-tsd-source attrs</i>\"]\n    vite --> server[\"Server Event Bus<br/><i>WebSocket + SSE transport</i>\"]\n    vite --> strip[\"Production Stripping<br/><i>Remove devtools on build</i>\"]\n    vite --> pipe[\"Console Piping<br/><i>Client ↔ Server logs</i>\"]\n    vite --> logs[\"Enhanced Logs<br/><i>Source location in console</i>\"]\n    vite --> market[\"Marketplace<br/><i>Install plugins via UI</i>\"]\n    vite --> connect[\"Connection Injection<br/><i>Port/host placeholders</i>\"]\n```\n\n### Go to Source\n\nThe \"Go to Source\" feature lets you click on any element in your browser and open its source file in your editor at the exact line where it's defined. It works by injecting `data-tsd-source` attributes into your components via an AST transformation during development. These attributes encode the file path and line number of each element.\n\nTo use it, activate the source inspector by holding the inspect hotkey (default: Shift+Alt+Ctrl/Meta). An overlay will highlight elements under your cursor and display their source location. Clicking on a highlighted element opens the corresponding file in your editor at the exact line, powered by `launch-editor` under the hood.\n\nFor a complete guide on configuration and usage, see the [Source Inspector](./source-inspector) docs.\n\n### Console Piping\n\nWhen enabled (default), `console.log()` and other console methods in the browser are piped to your terminal, and server-side console output appears in the browser console. This is particularly useful when debugging SSR or API routes — you see all logs in one place without switching between terminal and browser. Configure which log levels are piped via `consolePiping.levels`.\n\n### Enhanced Logs\n\nConsole logs are enhanced with clickable source locations. In the browser console, each log shows the file and line number where it originated. Click to open the source file in your editor. Enable/disable via the `enhancedLogs` config option.\n\n### Production Build Stripping\n\nBy default (`removeDevtoolsOnBuild: true`), the Vite plugin replaces all devtools imports with empty modules in production builds. This includes:\n- `@tanstack/react-devtools`\n- `@tanstack/vue-devtools`\n- `@tanstack/solid-devtools`\n- `@tanstack/preact-devtools`\n- `@tanstack/devtools`\n\nThis ensures zero devtools code reaches production. Set `removeDevtoolsOnBuild: false` to keep devtools in production (see [Production](./production) docs).\n\n### Plugin Marketplace\n\nThe Vite plugin enables the in-devtools plugin marketplace. When you browse available plugins in the devtools Settings tab and click \"Install\", the Vite plugin handles the npm/pnpm/yarn installation and automatically injects the plugin import into your devtools setup file. This only works during development with the Vite dev server running.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Vite Plugin\nid: vite-plugin\n---\n\nThe Vite Plugin for TanStack Devtools provides a seamless integration for using the devtools in your Vite-powered applications. With this plugin, you get complementary features on top of the\nexisting features built into the devtools like better console logs, server event bus, and enhanced debugging capabilities.\n\n## Installation\n\nTo add the devtools vite plugin you need to install it as a development dependency:\n\n```sh\nnpm install -D @tanstack/devtools-vite\n```\n\nThen add it as the *FIRST* plugin in your Vite config:\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools(),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\nAnd you're done!\n\n## Configuration\n\nYou can configure the devtools plugin by passing options to the `devtools` function:\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      // options here\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### eventBusConfig\n\n  Configuration for the event bus that the devtools use to communicate with the client\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      eventBusConfig: {\n        // port to run the event bus on\n        port: 1234,\n        // console log debug logs or not\n        debug: false,\n        // enables the server event bus (defaults to true), you can disable it if you're running devtools in something like storybook or vitest\n        enabled: true\n      },\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n\n```\n\n### editor\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] \n> `editor` is used as an escape hatch to implement your own go-to-source functionality if your system/editor does not work OOTB. We use `launch-editor` under the hood which supports a lot of editors but not all. If your editor is not supported you can implement your own version here. Here is the list of supported editors: https://github.com/yyx990803/launch-editor?tab=readme-ov-file#supported-editors\n\nThe open in editor configuration which has two fields, `name` and `open`,\n`name` is the name of your editor, and `open` is a function that opens the editor with the given file and line number. You can implement your version for your editor as follows:\n\n ```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      editor: {\n        name: 'VSCode',\n        open: async (path, lineNumber, columnNumber) => {\n          const { exec } = await import('node:child_process')\n          exec(\n            // or windsurf/cursor/webstorm\n            `code -g \"${(path).replaceAll('$', '\\\\$')}${lineNumber ? `:${lineNumber}` : ''}${columnNumber ? `:${columnNumber}` : ''}\"`,\n          )\n        },\n      },\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n\n```\n\n### enhancedLogs\n\n  Configuration for enhanced logging. Defaults to enabled.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      enhancedLogs: {\n        enabled: true\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### removeDevtoolsOnBuild\n\nWhether to remove devtools from the production build. Defaults to true.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      removeDevtoolsOnBuild: true\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### logging\n  Whether to log information to the console. Defaults to true.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      logging: true\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### injectSource\n\nConfiguration for source injection. Defaults to enabled.\n\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      injectSource: {\n        enabled: true,\n        ignore: {\n          // files to ignore source injection for\n          files: ['node_modules', /.*\\.test\\.(js|ts|jsx|tsx)$/],\n          // components to ignore source injection for\n          components: ['YourComponent', /.*Lazy$/],\n        },\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n### consolePiping\n\nConfiguration for bidirectional console piping between client and server. When enabled, console logs from the client will appear in your terminal, and server logs will appear in the browser console. Defaults to enabled.\n\n```ts\nimport { devtools } from '@tanstack/devtools-vite'\n\nexport default {\n  plugins: [\n    devtools({\n      consolePiping: {\n        // Whether to enable console piping (defaults to true)\n        enabled: true,\n        // Which console methods to pipe (defaults to all)\n        levels: ['log', 'warn', 'error', 'info', 'debug'],\n      }\n    }),\n    // ... rest of your plugins here\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n## Features\n\nThe Vite plugin is composed of several sub-plugins, each handling a specific concern:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    vite[\"@tanstack/devtools-vite\"]\n    vite --> source[\"Source Injection<br/><i>AST → data-tsd-source attrs</i>\"]\n    vite --> server[\"Server Event Bus<br/><i>WebSocket + SSE transport</i>\"]\n    vite --> strip[\"Production Stripping<br/><i>Remove devtools on build</i>\"]\n    vite --> pipe[\"Console Piping<br/><i>Client ↔ Server logs</i>\"]\n    vite --> logs[\"Enhanced Logs<br/><i>Source location in console</i>\"]\n    vite --> market[\"Marketplace<br/><i>Install plugins via UI</i>\"]\n    vite --> connect[\"Connection Injection<br/><i>Port/host placeholders</i>\"]\n```\n\n### Go to Source\n\nThe \"Go to Source\" feature lets you click on any element in your browser and open its source file in your editor at the exact line where it's defined. It works by injecting `data-tsd-source` attributes into your components via an AST transformation during development. These attributes encode the file path and line number of each element.\n\nTo use it, activate the source inspector by holding the inspect hotkey (default: Shift+Alt+Ctrl/Meta). An overlay will highlight elements under your cursor and display their source location. Clicking on a highlighted element opens the corresponding file in your editor at the exact line, powered by `launch-editor` under the hood.\n\nFor a complete guide on configuration and usage, see the [Source Inspector](./source-inspector) docs.\n\n### Console Piping\n\nWhen enabled (default), `console.log()` and other console methods in the browser are piped to your terminal, and server-side console output appears in the browser console. This is particularly useful when debugging SSR or API routes — you see all logs in one place without switching between terminal and browser. Configure which log levels are piped via `consolePiping.levels`.\n\n### Enhanced Logs\n\nConsole logs are enhanced with clickable source locations. In the browser console, each log shows the file and line number where it originated. Click to open the source file in your editor. Enable/disable via the `enhancedLogs` config option.\n\n### Production Build Stripping\n\nBy default (`removeDevtoolsOnBuild: true`), the Vite plugin replaces all devtools imports with empty modules in production builds. This includes:\n- `@tanstack/react-devtools`\n- `@tanstack/vue-devtools`\n- `@tanstack/solid-devtools`\n- `@tanstack/preact-devtools`\n- `@tanstack/devtools`\n\nThis ensures zero devtools code reaches production. Set `removeDevtoolsOnBuild: false` to keep devtools in production (see [Production](./production) docs).\n\n### Plugin Marketplace\n\nThe Vite plugin enables the in-devtools plugin marketplace. When you browse available plugins in the devtools Settings tab and click \"Install\", the Vite plugin handles the npm/pnpm/yarn installation and automatically injects the plugin import into your devtools setup file. This only works during development with the Vite dev server running.\n"}
